Calibration arc -- the scientific product of the experiment ladder
===================================================================
freeze tag: experiment-4-frozen (fe26dc8)

This record stands independent of whatever Experiment 4 produces.

The arc
-------

pre-evaluator:  computable premises surfaced as requests
                (instrument incomplete). Every request the ladder
                produced -- (coprime 8 15), (eq 21 (times 3 7)),
                (odd 4), (divides 3 14) -- was a decidable evaluation,
                not a theorem. Headline then: the first thing this
                machine runs out of is arithmetic, not number theory.

post-evaluator: computable premises close cold (DOMAIN_AXIOM) or
                refute (COUNTEREXAMPLE); only genuine theorem-shaped
                gaps reach the request compiler. Headline now: the
                machine does not run out of arithmetic at all -- it
                runs out of theorems.

consequence:    a computable request is a defect signal, not an
                operator chore. A residual that survives to
                `suggest dependencies` on the frozen build is
                theorem-shaped OR an evaluator-coverage gap: the
                filter guarantees only that nothing the wired
                evaluations (eq over times/plus/minus/pow, divides,
                coprime, odd/even, comparisons) could decide gets
                through. A ground decidable predicate OUTSIDE that
                coverage -- say (prime 91) with no primality edge --
                would surface looking theorem-shaped while being
                computation. The operator distinguishes the two by
                asking whether the premise is ground and decidable;
                a covered-but-surfaced request is defect eight. On
                this substrate, evaluation and theorem-need are
                separable exactly as far as the evaluator's coverage,
                and the separation is mechanical within it.

Incident, kept verbatim
-----------------------

SinglePremiseGoalSeededTest was authored on the goal (congruent 9 2 5)
with the law (divides ?m (minus ?a ?b)) -> (congruent ?a ?b ?m). The
goal is false: 9 - 2 = 7 and 5 does not divide 7. When the ground
evaluator landed, the test failed -- not by crash but by refutation:
the machine evaluated (divides 5 (minus 9 2)) to false, recorded it
COUNTEREXAMPLE, and declined to request it. The test's author had
asserted a falsehood; the instrument contradicted its own test suite
on a matter of arithmetic fact. The test now runs on symbolic
constants and the incident stays here: the evaluator is a decision
procedure, not a lookup dressed up.

Defect ledger, one line each, all found by running
--------------------------------------------------

1. prefix-only premise matching -- a rule with its failing premise
   first produced no foothold (found by the coprime ladder)
2. fired instantiations shadowed genuine partial matches for other
   instantiations (found by the second ladder problem)
3. attempt records lacked the goal-to-rule substitution -- schematic
   holes instead of concrete premises (found by the Step 5 probe)
4. fuel bounded rounds, not work -- one generative law exhausted the
   host stack inside a round (found by teaching Divides)
5. one-premise rules blocked on their only premise had no foothold --
   conclusion-to-goal unification now is the foothold (found by
   teaching Congruent)
6. conclusions with free variables could never fire forward -- the
   bounded search gained goal-directed firing (found by exponent
   algebra)
7. host runtime handle guarded with IdentityCompare -- crashed on the
   first real `load theorem packs` (found by the freeze manifest run;
   tag removed before first use and re-cut on the fix)

